<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="glutathione-mediated detoxification II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6842" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Glutathion plays an important role in cellular redox control and detoxification of heavy metals, electrophilic xenobiotics, and toxic side products of normal metabolism. The detoxification process starts with the formation of |FRAME: S-Substituted-Glutathione &quot;glutathione-&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-conjugates&quot;| in the cytosol, which is catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-2.5.1.18|. The subsequent degradation of these conjugates may occur in either the cytosol or the vacuole. In |FRAME: TAX-3702|, the degradation routes are different in the two subcellular locations. In the vacuole, the initial step is catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-3.4.19.13|, which removes the N-terminal glutamate residue, similar to the pathway found in animals |CITS: [17561001]|. In the cytosol, however, the process starts with removal of the C-terminal glycine by |FRAME: EC-3.4.17.25| |CITS: [12620355][18522943]|, a process that has not been been reported from animals. The identity of the dipeptidase(s) that act next to remove the &amp;gamma;-glutamyl residue has not been reported, although this activity may be catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-3.4.19.13|. A similar forked pathway has also been reported from the yeast |FRAME: TAX-4932|, although both routes appear to be associated with the vacuole. Following conjugation to glutathione, the xenobiotic is exported to the vacuole, where either the glycine residue is removed by the vacuolar serine carboxypeptidases |FRAME: G3O-29093-MONOMER| (|FRAME: G3O-29093|) and |FRAME: YMR297W-MONOMER| (|FRAME: YMR297W|), or is catabolized by the action of the vacuole-membrane-bound &amp;gamma;-glutamyltransferase encoded by the |FRAME: YLR299W| gene |CITS: [19897216]|. A yeast triple mutant, lacking functional |FRAME: G3O-29093|, |FRAME: YMR297W|, and |FRAME: YLR299W|, completely lost the ability to metabolize |FRAME: CPD-21160|. The expression of the Arabidopsis phytochelatin synthase |FRAME: AT5G44070| in the triple knockout mutant could restore this ability, resulted in the turnover of |FRAME: CPD-21160| to |FRAME: CPD-21164|, as observed in plants |CITS: [19897216]|. Both routes lead to a common intermediate, |FRAME: S-Substituted-L-Cysteines &quot;L-cysteine-&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-conjugate&quot;|. Less is known about further degradation of this intermediate. In animals Cys-&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-conjugates are acetylated by |FRAME: EC-2.3.1.80|, to form &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-acetyl-Cys-S-conjugates, known as |FRAME: Mercapturates mercapturates|. However, this activity has not been detected in plants. Instead, plants appear to catalyze the malonylation of the Cys-&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-conjugates |CITS: [18522943]|. Although no genes have been correlated with this activity yet, potential candidates include a group of largely uncharacterized malonyltransferases that have been reported |CITS: [Benichou95]|. A second, minor, route for |FRAME: S-Substituted-L-Cysteines &quot;L-cysteine-&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-conjugate&quot;| degradation has been described from Arabidopsis cell culture. A small portion of the Cys-&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-conjugates was converted to thiols by |FRAME: EC-4.4.1.13| , followed by the action of an unknown methyltransferase to form methyl thiolethers |CITS: [18522943]|.</Description>
